**Key Lemma (Erdős, 1959).** *For any integers $g \geq 1$ and $k \geq 1$, there exists a finite graph with girth $> g$ and chromatic number $> k$.*

*Proof.* This is the classical probabilistic construction. Take $G(n,p)$ with $p = n^{-1+1/(2g)}$. The expected number of cycles of length $\leq g$ is $O(n^{1/2})$, so by Markov's inequality, with high probability there are at most $O(n^{1/2})$ short cycles. The independence number is $O(n^{1-1/(2g)} \log n)$ with high probability, giving $\chi \geq n^{1/(2g)}/(3\log n) > k$ for $n$ large enough. With positive probability both events hold; removing one edge per short cycle yields girth $> g$ and chromatic number $> k$. $\square$

